@*
    An AL object kind as it appears in a results-grid cell: the design system's
    .okind badge (the same two letters and tint the explorer tree draws) with
    the kind word beside it.

    One component rather than the markup twice, because the release grid and
    the module grid list the same objects one page apart, and they were already
    disagreeing - the release grid had a tinted pill and the module grid had a
    bare word. The letters double as the search box's kind prefixes; see
    ObjectKindGlyph.

    A legacy C/AL kind (form, dataport) has no prefix and so no badge. It gets
    the word alone, which is honest - inventing letters no search accepts is
    not.
*@

<span class="okind-cell">
    @if (Glyph.Length > 0)
    {
        <span class="okind @ObjectKindGlyph.TintClass(Kind)">@Glyph</span>
    }
    <span>@Kind</span>
</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>The raw kind as stored, e.g. <c>tableextension</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Kind { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    private string Glyph => ObjectKindGlyph.For(Kind);
}
